00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.550
And particularly, right,
what I find interesting

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:50.640
is you mentioned that
Indian users are different.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.678
Could you share a few
points around that?

00:02:53.678 --> 00:02:54.470
RUSHABH VASA: Sure.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.470
So Indian users and
content consumption habits

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:58.350
are different.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:01.280
For example, most of our
listeners come from tier two

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.740
and tier three cities of India.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.230
And since internet
connectivity was an issue,

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.540
we had to make sure
the listeners have

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.000
seamless streaming
experience while listening

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.760
to their favorite podcasts
even in low bandwidth.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.140
Also we found a few interesting
things, like a lot of users

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:21.500
have preference to download
the podcast and listen offline.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.420
When we tried to
figure out the reason,

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:27.170
we came to know that users are
conscious and conservative when

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:28.760
it comes to using data.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:31.610
Since podcasts are new
in India, discovery

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:33.020
is also a big challenge.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:36.560
That's where we use the data
and build a basic recommendation

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.460
mechanism where users get a
personalized notification based

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:42.380
on their historic
listening patterns

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.870
and also make it
culturally relevant,

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:48.110
like sending notifications for
Hanuman Chalisa on Saturday

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:49.010
