00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:03.830
how you built this
on Google Cloud.

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:08.180
So could you guide us through
a dive into a user's journey,

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:10.580
or specifically,
what happens when

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:13.790
a user wants to listen
to a particular podcast

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:15.470
and they click Play?

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:16.220
RUSHABH VASA: Yes.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.750
As I said, our distribution
is our strength.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.810
It requires a
seamless experience

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.600
for our listeners to stream
their favorite podcasts

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:24.930
on all platforms.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.710
That's where our robust
architecture on Google Cloud

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:30.750
platform helps us achieve
this performance at scale.

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:33.230
So just to explain you
about the architecture,

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:36.980
whenever a user clicks on a
play button or sends a request.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:39.724
Let's say they've opened
a website Aawaz.com.

00:04:39.724 --> 00:04:43.610
That goes to Cloud DNS where
we resolve that domain name

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:47.240
and then through a Cloud Run,
we load a front-end website

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:48.120
for the user.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.180
And then whenever a
user clicks or selects

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.100
any particular
episode that he wants

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:54.248
to listen to on a
podcast, that again

00:04:54.248 --> 00:04:55.790
requests come through
a load balancer

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:59.420
because there are millions
of streams coming every day.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:00.800
So through load
balancer it goes,

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.320
the request goes back
to the Compute Engine

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:06.290
and that's where the backend
APIs are managed through that.

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:11.330
Our Cloud Storage gives them the
right episode file, the image,

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:12.380
and thumbnails.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:15.900
Then again, that information
sent back to the user.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.100
That's where the user gets
to listen to his podcast.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:22.940
Let's say if the request
comes from the backend API,

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.640
then again the Cloud
handles the partner's API

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:29.460
that we have built and those
APIs are then again processed

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:33.165
to our Compute Engine and then
the same process is followed

00:05:33.165 --> 00:05:34.790
and users get to
listen to the podcast.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:40.920
With the help of
this architecture

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:45.120
it was possible to scale
from 10,000 streams on Day 1

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.948
to more than 90 million
streams monthly.

00:05:47.948 --> 00:05:48.990
ROMIN IRANI: Interesting.

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.750
Thank you for taking us
through that user journey

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.690
and what happens from an
architecture point of view.

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:56.940
Now, looking at
the scale, and I'm

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.130
